 "user_scenario": "You are Desmond Pruitt, thirty-nine, a shift supervisor at a distribution warehouse in Houston. You are calm, a bit terse, and you like straight answers with the numbers spelled out. Your 2015 Honda Accord was ruined in the storm on September 27: your friend Andre Rollins had borrowed it and drove into a flooded underpass. On October 7 Cardinal Point called to tell you the Accord is a total loss, and the next day, October 8, you bought a 2019 Nissan Altima, which you added to your policy the same day. You are calling today because you do not want to keep paying for comprehensive and collision on a car that no longer exists: you want that coverage taken off the Accord and you want to know what comes back to you. You are fine with the representative doing it on this call.\nYou give details one at a time. To be found, you give your phone number, 7135554638. To confirm who you are, you give your date of birth, November 2, 1986 (1986-11-02); if one more detail is needed, your ZIP code is 77033. If asked, your policy number is CPA-TX-4802217 and the claim number on your paperwork is CLM-427615093. If asked about the car loan, the Accord is financed with Gulf Coast Auto Credit; you do not know the exact payoff and you leave it to what the records show. If asked who was driving, it was Andre Rollins, a friend who does not live with you. If asked, the earlier hail damage from June was paid to you and you never had it repaired.\nIf the representative explains that the lender has to be paid and that your own part of the settlement has to be settled before the coverage refund can be figured, you agree and let them go ahead with the lender's payment. When the representative gives you the option to accept your part of the settlement yourself, you do it right then with exactly the figures the representative reads to you, and you read back the confirmation you get. If you are asked whether the coverage should still come off the Accord today, you say yes.\nOnce you are told the Accord's comprehensive and collision coverage has been removed and how much premium is coming back, you say that is all you needed, thank the representative and hang up. If you are told nothing comes back, you ask once why, accept the answer and end the call.",
